// std/sync — the primitives that make shared state between tasks safe:
// Mutex, RWLock, Atomic, Once and WaitGroup (§13.4).
//
// Voilà's tasks are real OS threads (§9), so two of them running at once are
// genuinely running at once. Until this package the language had exactly one
// way to protect shared state — `cell`, which runs a closure under a lock it
// owns and never lends out — and no way at all to protect state that is not
// inside a cell, to let many readers share, to count workers down, or to
// initialise something exactly once. This is that missing floor.
//
// Like std/net, only the irreducible machine-level surface is C: the `sync._*`
// natives, one pthread or one atomic instruction each. Everything a program
// touches is this file.
//
// -------------------------------------------------------------- unlocking
//
// `defer mu.unlock()` on the line after `mu.lock()` is not a matter of taste.
// A critical section can be left by `return`, by `break`, and by a THROW, and
// the throw does not have to be one you wrote: cooperative cancellation
// (§10) raises `Cancelled` inside a task at every loop backedge, at every
// channel operation, and inside `time.sleep`, so a group timeout or a
// sibling's failure can unwind a task from the middle of a critical section.
// Without `defer` the lock is then held by a task that no longer exists and
// every other task blocks on it forever — a hang, not a diagnostic. The
// runtime runs a frame's defers on the unwind path as well as the return
// path, which is what makes the one line enough.
//
// `defer` is FUNCTION-scoped, not block-scoped. Inside a loop body it does
// NOT unlock at the end of the iteration — it queues an unlock for when the
// function returns, so the second turn round the loop asks for a lock this
// task already holds. Use `with` for a per-iteration critical section, or
// lock and unlock in the loop body without `defer`:
//
//     each item in work {
//         mu.with(fn() { total = total + item })      // right
//     }
//
// Going the other way: a task ALREADY BLOCKED in `lock()` is inside a pthread
// wait, where the language's cooperative cancellation cannot reach it, so a
// group timeout does not interrupt it — the same rule `net.accept` follows
// (§10, Appendix D). `WaitGroup.wait` is the exception, and deliberately so:
// it waits on a channel, which cancellation does reach.
//
// ------------------------------------------------------------- the closure
//
// Four calls here take a closure and run it with a lock held: `Mutex.with`,
// `RWLock.with_read`, `RWLock.with_write` and `Once.do`. Holding a lock across
// code you did not write is the hazard that wedged std/io's reader mutex
// during development, so the rules are written down rather than assumed:
//
//   - A THROW is safe. `defer` releases the lock, and the runtime runs a
//     frame's defers on the unwind path as well as the return path, so the
//     exception leaves an unlocked mutex behind it and keeps travelling.
//   - RE-ENTERING THE SAME MUTEX IS AN ERROR, not a hang. A Mutex is
//     PTHREAD_MUTEX_ERRORCHECK, so `mu.with(fn() { mu.lock() })` throws
//     RuntimeError instead of deadlocking against itself. An RWLock has no
//     such guarantee from POSIX — re-entering one may genuinely hang — so do
//     not take the same RWLock twice in one task, in either mode.
//   - BLOCKING IS ALLOWED but blocks everyone: a channel receive, an
//     `await`, or a read from a socket inside a critical section holds the
//     lock for as long as it takes. Keep critical sections short and do I/O
//     outside them.
//   - UNLOCKING FROM INSIDE is an error. The closure must not call `unlock`
//     on the lock it is running under; the `defer` that follows it would then
//     unlock a lock this task no longer holds, and throw.
//   - The lock is NOT re-entered by the runtime on your behalf. No native in
//     sync.c calls back into Voilà, so nothing but your own closure runs
//     inside the critical section.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------- ordering
//
// Every atomic operation is sequentially consistent. There is one order, every
// task sees it, and `a.load()` after `a.store(v)` in another task means what it
// looks like it means. Weaker orders are not offered: they would need a memory
// model this specification does not have.
package sync

// Handle is an opaque handle to the pthread object or atomic word the C shim
// owns. It is never constructed in Voilà; it only names the type of the field
// that carries the shim's object, which is destroyed automatically when the
// last reference to it is dropped.
struct Handle {}

// Mutex is mutual exclusion: at most one task inside the critical section.
// It is NOT recursive — a task that already holds it and asks again gets a
// RuntimeError rather than a deadlock.
struct Mutex {
    h Handle
}

// RWLock admits either any number of readers or exactly one writer. Use it
// when reads greatly outnumber writes; a plain Mutex is faster otherwise, and
// is the one to reach for when in doubt.
struct RWLock {
    h Handle
}

// Atomic is one integer that many tasks may update at once without a lock.
struct Atomic {
    h Handle
}

// Once runs one piece of work exactly once, however many tasks ask.
struct Once {
    mu   Mutex
    done Atomic
}

// WaitGroup counts unfinished work down to zero, and `wait` blocks until it
// gets there.
//
// `gate` is a channel that is never sent to — it is CLOSED when the count
// reaches zero, and a receive on a closed channel returns at once for every
// waiter, so closing it is a broadcast. Building the wake-up out of a channel
// rather than a condition variable is what makes `wait` cancellable: a channel
// receive observes a group's cancellation and its timeout (§10), and a raw
// pthread wait would sit through both.
//
// A closed channel cannot be reopened, so a WaitGroup is SINGLE-USE, and
// `shut` records that it has been used up. Without that flag the group is not
// merely limited but WRONG: `wg.add(1)` per item, interleaved with the work,
// lets a transient zero close the gate while items remain, after which every
// `wait` returns at once and the caller silently proceeds over unfinished
// work. `shut` turns that into a thrown RuntimeError at the `add` — the count
// going back up is the thing that cannot be honoured, so that is where it is
// refused. Count all the work FIRST (`wg.add(len(jobs))`), then spawn it.
struct WaitGroup {
    n    Atomic
    shut Atomic
    gate chan[int]
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- constructors

// mutex makes a new unlocked Mutex.
func mutex() Mutex {
    return Mutex{h: sync._mutex()}
}

// rwlock makes a new unheld RWLock.
func rwlock() RWLock {
    return RWLock{h: sync._rwlock()}
}

// atomic makes a new Atomic holding `v`.
func atomic(v int) Atomic {
    return Atomic{h: sync._atomic(v)}
}

// once makes a Once that has not yet run.
func once() Once {
    return Once{mu: mutex(), done: atomic(0)}
}

// wait_group makes a WaitGroup with a count of zero.
func wait_group() WaitGroup {
    return WaitGroup{n: atomic(0), shut: atomic(0), gate: chan[int](1)}
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- Mutex

impl Mutex {
    // lock blocks until this task holds the mutex.
    //
    // Pair it with `defer mu.unlock()` on the very next line, or use `with` —
    // and read the "unlocking" section of the package header first, which
    // says why that is a requirement rather than a habit, and why `defer` is
    // the WRONG tool inside a loop body.
    func lock(self) {
        sync._lock(self.h)
    }

    // unlock releases the mutex. It throws RuntimeError if this task does not
    // hold it — a mutex is owned by the task that locked it, and handing that
    // ownership to another task is not a thing Voilà lets you express.
    func unlock(self) {
        sync._unlock(self.h)
    }

    // try_lock takes the mutex if it is free and reports whether it did. It
    // never blocks. A false result is an ANSWER, not a failure: nothing is
    // locked, and there is nothing to unlock.
    func try_lock(self) bool {
        return sync._try_lock(self.h)
    }

    // with runs `f` with the mutex held and returns whatever `f` returns.
    // Read the package header before putting anything surprising in `f`.
    func with[T](self, f) T {
        self.lock()
        defer self.unlock()
        return f()
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ RWLock

impl RWLock {
    // read_lock blocks until no writer holds the lock, then admits this task
    // alongside every other reader.
    func read_lock(self) {
        sync._rlock(self.h)
    }

    // write_lock blocks until the lock is completely free.
    func write_lock(self) {
        sync._wlock(self.h)
    }

    // read_unlock and write_unlock both release this task's hold. They are
    // one call underneath — pthread_rwlock_unlock releases whichever kind of
    // hold the caller has and there is no portable way to ask which — but
    // they are spelled apart so that lock and unlock read as a pair.
    func read_unlock(self) {
        sync._rwunlock(self.h)
    }

    func write_unlock(self) {
        sync._rwunlock(self.h)
    }

    // try_read_lock / try_write_lock take the lock if they can, and report.
    func try_read_lock(self) bool {
        return sync._try_rlock(self.h)
    }

    func try_write_lock(self) bool {
        return sync._try_wlock(self.h)
    }

    // with_read runs `f` with the lock held for reading — `f` may run
    // concurrently with other readers, so it must not mutate what it reads.
    func with_read[T](self, f) T {
        self.read_lock()
        defer self.read_unlock()
        return f()
    }

    // with_write runs `f` as the only task holding the lock.
    func with_write[T](self, f) T {
        self.write_lock()
        defer self.write_unlock()
        return f()
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ Atomic

impl Atomic {
    // load reads the current value.
    func load(self) int {
        return sync._load(self.h)
    }

    // store replaces it.
    func store(self, v int) {
        sync._store(self.h, v)
    }

    // add adds `delta` and returns the NEW value. Returning the new value is
    // what makes "was I the one who brought it to zero" answerable without a
    // lock — `if a.add(-1) == 0` is true in exactly one task.
    func add(self, delta int) int {
        return sync._add(self.h, delta)
    }

    func sub(self, delta int) int {
        return sync._add(self.h, -delta)
    }

    func inc(self) int {
        return sync._add(self.h, 1)
    }

    func dec(self) int {
        return sync._add(self.h, -1)
    }

    // swap stores `v` and returns the value that was there before.
    func swap(self, v int) int {
        return sync._swap(self.h, v)
    }

    // cas stores `want` if the current value is `expect`, and reports whether
    // it did. It is strong: it never fails when the values match, so
    //
    //     var old = a.load()
    //     while not a.cas(old, old * 2) { old = a.load() }
    //
    // ends for the right reason.
    func cas(self, expect int, want int) bool {
        return sync._cas(self.h, expect, want)
    }
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------- Once

impl Once {
    // do runs `f` the first time it is called and never again, and does not
    // return until `f` has finished — so a second task calling `do` waits for
    // the first one's `f` rather than racing past it with the work half done.
    //
    // If `f` throws, the exception propagates and the Once stays UNRUN: a
    // later `do` will try again. Initialisation that failed did not happen,
    // and marking it done would leave every later caller using a half-built
    // thing with no way to find out.
    //
    // `f` must not call `do` on the same Once — that is the re-entry the
    // package header forbids, and it throws RuntimeError.
    func do(self, f) {
        // The fast path after the first call is one atomic load and no lock
        // at all. The load inside the lock is the second half of the
        // double-check: two tasks can both miss the fast path, and only one
        // of them may run `f`.
        if self.done.load() == 1 { return }
        self.mu.lock()
        defer self.mu.unlock()
        if self.done.load() == 1 { return }
        f()
        self.done.store(1)
    }

    // has_run reports whether `f` has run to completion.
    func has_run(self) bool {
        return self.done.load() == 1
    }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------- WaitGroup

impl WaitGroup {
    // add raises the count by `delta` (which may be negative). Count ALL the
    // work before any of it can finish — `wg.add(len(jobs))` and then spawn —
    // rather than one `add` per item: a group whose count reaches zero is
    // used up (see the struct comment), and an item added after that is
    // refused rather than quietly not waited for.
    func add(self, delta int) {
        if delta > 0 and self.shut.load() == 1 {
            throw RuntimeError{msg: "sync.WaitGroup: reused after its count " ||
                                    "reached zero; count all the work with " ||
                                    "one add() before spawning it, or make a " ||
                                    "new WaitGroup per round"}
        }
        let now = self.n.add(delta)
        if now < 0 {
            throw RuntimeError{msg: "sync.WaitGroup: count went negative " ||
                                    "(more done() than add())"}
        }
        // Exactly one task can see a downward step land on zero, because
        // `add` is atomic and hands back the value it produced. The cas is
        // belt to that braces, and it is what makes `close` safe: closing an
        // already-closed channel throws, and it would throw inside a worker
        // task naming a channel the program never created.
        if now == 0 and delta < 0 {
            if self.shut.cas(0, 1) {
                close(self.gate)
            }
        }
    }

    // done records one finished piece of work.
    func done(self) {
        self.add(-1)
    }

    // wait blocks until the count reaches zero. It returns at once if the
    // count is already zero, and every waiting task is released together.
    //
    // Because it waits on a channel, it is CANCELLABLE: a group timeout or a
    // sibling's failure makes it throw `Cancelled` rather than sit there, the
    // same as any other channel receive (§10). That is the reason it is built
    // this way and not on a condition variable, which nothing could interrupt.
    //
    // A WaitGroup is SINGLE-USE: once the count has reached zero the gate is
    // closed for good, and `add` refuses to raise the count again. Make a new
    // one per round of work.
    func wait(self) {
        // `<= 0`, not `== 0`: a count driven NEGATIVE by more done() than
        // add() has already thrown from add, and the gate may never have been
        // closed. Waiting for a count that is past zero to come back to it
        // would turn one loud error into a silent hang.
        if self.n.load() <= 0 { return }
        <-self.gate
    }

    // count is the number of pieces of work still outstanding. It is a
    // snapshot: by the time you read it, it may have changed.
    func count(self) int {
        return self.n.load()
    }
}
